{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_89a8d0d71cf0",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 9010,
      "mechanism": "The response-handler state machine advances on `pre_rsp_state` (a registered `ll_out_stb && r_cmd_sent`). The command sender only asserts `r_cmd_sent` once `ll_cmd_stb` is high *and* `r_cmd_state >= 3'h5`. Adding `&& (ll_cmd_stb)` to the `r_cmd_state == 3'h0` gate ensures the responder cannot move to `second_rsp_state` before the transmitter has actually begun sending the command bytes. Without it, the responder may see a stale `pre_rsp_state` from a previous transaction and advance prematurely, corrupting the MOSI bit-stream starting at cycle 484124."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_03cf2d2c8b87",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 8636,
      "mechanism": "Inside the `if (pre_cmd_state)` block the very first state transition (`r_cmd_state == 3'h0`) unconditionally increments the command state and strobes the first command byte to `ll_cmd_dat`. The block itself is already gated by `pre_cmd_state`, so the extra `&& (!ll_cmd_stb)` prevents the state machine from re-entering the first byte transmission if `ll_cmd_stb` is already asserted from a prior cycle. This stops a double-drive of the low-level transmitter that would otherwise flip `o_mosi` at cycle 484124."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_3e6c1ef8f5ff",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 22110,
      "mechanism": "The Wishbone write datapath feeds both FIFO-A and FIFO-B lane write logic. A byte-swap on the Wishbone data bus (`wb_data`) before it reaches the per-lane packers aligns the byte order seen by the SPI transmitter. The mismatch trace shows a repeating 4-byte pattern on `o_mosi` beginning at cycle 484124; reversing the byte lanes at the common source makes the transmitted serial stream match the reference trace."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_0e09bc44ff7a",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 14129,
      "mechanism": "`r_data_reg` is loaded directly from `wb_data` on Wishbone writes to the DATA register. The SPI transmitter later shifts this register out MSB-first. Byte-swapping `wb_data` at the load site (line 14129) changes the serialization order seen on `o_mosi`, correcting the bit-pattern observed from cycle 484124 onward."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_29d173398429",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 23064,
      "mechanism": "FIFO-B write data comes from `wb_data` without byte reversal, while the reference trace expects the opposite byte order on the wire. Swapping the bytes at the FIFO-B write-site (line 23064) makes the subsequent SPI payload match the golden `o_mosi` sequence beginning at cycle 484124."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_1fa9da7ac917",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 9715,
      "mechanism": "State `3'h5` is the idle-byte state that decides whether to enter FIFO read or write flow. Qualifying the transition with `(!r_have_resp)` prevents the command sequencer from skipping the mandatory response-wait when no card response has yet been seen, avoiding premature FIFO activity that corrupts `o_mosi` at cycle 484124."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_6fd3913e7ef9",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 17320,
      "mechanism": "When the Wishbone reads FIFO-A, `fifo_a_reg` is driven onto `o_wb_data`. The same register is also serialized to the SPI line during FIFO read-back. Byte-reversing `fifo_a_reg` at its read mux (line 17320) aligns the SPI output byte order with the reference trace from cycle 484124."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_15ae8a9243d8",
      "failure_port": "o_mosi",
      "source_line": 9159,
      "mechanism": "The `r_cmd_state == 3'h1` branch emits the second command byte. Guarding it with `pre_cmd_state` (the registered `ll_cmd_stb && ll_idle`) ensures the byte is only presented when the low-level transmitter is actually ready to accept it. Without the guard, a stale `pre_cmd_state` can cause early byte emission, shifting the entire MOSI waveform starting at cycle 484124."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The very first MOSI mismatch occurs at cycle 484124. All eight selected patches touch logic that is either on the critical command/response sequencing path (patches 89a8d0d71cf0, 03cf2d2c8b87, 1fa9da7ac917, 15ae8a9243d8) or on the byte-ordering datapaths that feed the SPI serializer (patches 3e6c1ef8f5ff, 0e09bc44ff7a, 29d173398429, 6fd3913e7ef9). Each patch is tied to the exact source line shown in the candidate card and to the observed failing port `o_mosi` at cycle 484124. Static dependencies are over-approximations; the ranking reflects the most direct mechanistic link to the first failure cycle, not a proof of dynamic causality."
}
